    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d301">

                <pb facs="0513" n="227" xml:id="pg227"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.857Su8/46a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Sharp</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-04-12T16:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 12, 1916, 4 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1468. <hi rendition="#italic">Sussex</hi> case. How long after <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Sussex</hi> arrived at Boulogne did Lieutenant
                        Smith and Major Logan make their
                    examination? What opportunity had persons freely to visit the vessel or to strew
                    fragments of metal about? Was there, in the opinion of
                        Smith and Logan or of other
                    persons whom you may consult, any opportunity or likelihood of their having been
                    imposed upon as to the fragments of metal picked up on board? Were two screws
                    damaged by explosion, and if so, in what way?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
